WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
(inquisitive music)
- This is the Terran 1,

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.140
the first rocket that's
almost entirely 3D printed.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.390
It was built by Relativity Space,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.430
a startup that is developing a new

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.910
potentially revolutionary
way to manufacture rockets

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.820
using the biggest metal
3D printers ever made.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.473
- When I started the company,
I thought there was a

00:00:19.473 --> 00:00:22.530
50/50 chance within three
months we figured out

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.850
3D printing a rocket was

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.370
just not gonna be viable at all.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.710
- [Narrator] Although Relativity
has made impressive strides

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.150
in the field of 3D printing,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.430
it's never launched a rocket into space,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.100
a notoriously difficult
feat for any company

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.700
let alone one that uses an
untested manufacturing method.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.500
Yet that lack of a track record

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.750
hasn't diminished customer's interest.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.420
As the date of Relativity
first launch approaches,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.880
the company says the
Terran 1 is one of the most

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.673
pre-sold rockets in history before launch.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.840
Today's global commercial
space industry is estimated

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.780
to be worth over $350 billion.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.380
- Weightlessness. Oh Jesus.
(man moaning)

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.880
- [Narrator] Although space tourism gets

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.840
most of the headlines,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.810
it's only a tiny slice of
the overall market compared

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.240
to satellite manufacturing and services,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.910
which make up almost 50% of the sector.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.590
According to a city group report,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.600
the satellite market
could more than double

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.080
to 697 billion by 2040.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.947
- [Launch Counter] Three, two, one

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.300
- [Narrator] To cater to this
growing demand for satellites

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.360
a crop of private aerospace
startups have begun developing

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.820
new technologies to
drive down launch prices

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.520
and capture a share of the market.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.380
Relativity Space, which was founded

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.080
in 2016 by rocket engineer, Tim Ellis,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.910
is one of the most
closely watched companies

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.290
in the launch sector.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.690
- The first week of starting the company

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.400
and actually the first email I sent

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.850
from my Relativity Space
email account was a cold email

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:48.930
to Mark Cuban.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.180
We were raising about
half a million dollars,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.180
asked him for $100,000 and then I think

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.370
about 10 minutes later, he replied back

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.770
and said he would give
us the entire thing.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.690
- [Narrator] From those
auspicious beginnings

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.090
Relativity expanded rapidly to include

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.330
over 1,000 employees and
multiple production test

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.640
and launch sites across the country.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.460
Relativity is also just
the fourth company to

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.763
ever receive a dedicated
launchpad at Cape Canaveral.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.310
For the past 60 years,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.430
rocket manufacturing has
relied on a labor intensive

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.490
and time consuming process
involving the assembly

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.650
of thousands of parts by hand.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.510
- We have traditionally sent rockets

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.660
into orbit by designing
them very carefully

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.780
on the ground and going
through several iterations

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.470
of every single component of the rocket

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.150
starting from the engines all the way

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.130
to the design of the vehicle itself.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.760
And rockets work at the
limits of their capabilities

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.700
and that's why it takes so long.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.410
- [Narrator] Relativity
is looking to shake

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.620
up that traditional model by
using advanced 3D printers

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.560
and artificial intelligence
to produce rocket engines

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.463
and other complex parts in
60 days rather than years.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.940
- This is our second generation printer.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.040
We have a third generation
that's about twice

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.120
as big as this

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.340
and then a fourth generation
that's even bigger than that.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.220
So this one's an older
version, but it's still

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.610
one that we're using to produce
flight hardware actively.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.500
- [Narrator] Traditionally,
the different metal parts

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.090
of a rocket have been assembled together

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.310
by shaping, milling and welding.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.760
But at Relativity engineers
program a robot printer

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.330
to deposit layers of the
company's proprietary metal alloys

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:28.830
one on top of another.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.410
- So we have our own material
science team that develops

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.540
our own custom aluminum alloys,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.450
which is how we can 3D print
a rocket that's strong enough

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.100
and has good enough quality.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.660
We use a combination of plasma
arc discharge and lasers

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.120
to melt and then collect data

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.070
on this aluminum wire as it's fed

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.800
in to the print head at
the end of a robot arm.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.780
- [Narrator] To optimize
its rockets designs,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.850
Relativity leverages algorithms

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.550
and machine learning to
refine each iteration.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.460
- Since you're 3D printing
it, you can build almost

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.370
anything that the computer
algorithm comes up with.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.770
So you do, much like nature,

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.860
end up with these
evolutionary looking parts.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.920
It's really because it's
a computer algorithm

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.833
using physics to determine
what the part looks like.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.040
So this is a dome

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.023
for the new rocket we're
developing, Terran R.

00:04:20.023 --> 00:04:21.540
- [Narrator] The Terran R is the

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.390
second rocket Relativity
is developing compared

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.280
to its smaller cousin, the Terran 1,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.010
which has about a 2,700 pound payload.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:33.000
The Terran R has a payload
capacity of about 44,000 pounds.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.410
Relativity says it expects to
launch the Terran R in 2024.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.470
- So we intentionally have
this very wavy pattern

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.720
so that makes it much stiffer.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:43.890
It's really starting to show

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.960
with 3D printing what you can do

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.890
that you really cannot do traditionally.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:51.450
With normal manufacturing
building something like this,

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.590
it would be very challenging.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.870
You would have to have a massive press

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.030
and then you also couldn't
vary the thickness

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.580
of the dome in all the
different locations easily

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.200
like we've been able to do here.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.570
So we really can cut out quite a few steps

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.730
by printing something like this.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.380
So really 3D printing

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.170
and the approach we're taking
is gonna get even better,

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.990
the larger things that we build.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.450
- [Narrator] Rocket manufacturers
have typically relied

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.970
on complex supply chains
in which different parts

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.850
of the rocket are made by
different subcontractors.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.250
Relativity turns that model on its head

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.020
by bringing everything in-house

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.540
with a vertically integrated
supply chain intended

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.640
to make the company a one stop shop

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.590
for sending rockets into space.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:33.570
- We had to simultaneously build a

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.880
brand new manufacturing technology,

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.040
the world's largest metal 3D printers

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.170
and build our own rocket.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.850
We have our own rocket engines.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.150
We develop our own flight
software, all the electronics

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.120
and computers that control
the rocket, the structures,

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.590
the test site, the launch site.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.110
But the advantage of
that I is there really is

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.790
not off the shelf technology.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.160
You can just go buy and plug in.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.270
Building everything from
scratch lets us also cut out

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.120
kind of middlemen as far as suppliers

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.380
and other companies that
frankly would just slow us down.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.690
- [Narrator] Relativity isn't
the first rocket company

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.620
to incorporate 3D printing
into its manufacturing process

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.110
but when it comes to 3D
printing and rockets,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.960
no other company matches
the ambition of Relativity.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.400
According to the company, the Terran 1 is

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.670
85% 3D printed by mass.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.850
For its next rocket, the
Terran R, Relativity's goal is

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.720
for it to be 95% 3D printed by mass.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.660
Last year Relativity
announced it was also planning

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.500
to launch the first commercial
mission to Mars in 2024.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.200
- It's a really ambitious
but important mission

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.930
and despite the private
space industry this year

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:46.590
being two decades old, there's
really only two companies,

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.210
SpaceX and now Relativity,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.983
that are working on this mission.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.950
- [Narrator] Relativity's
biggest test to date is expected

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.680
to take place in February
when the company will attempt

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.660
to launch the Terran 1 into orbit.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.160
- Rockets work at the limit

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.467
of what is possible to do with materials

00:07:01.467 --> 00:07:04.920
and when we introduce a
new manufacturing technique

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.560
which is what has been
done by using 3D printer

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.280
we introduce a set of
unknowns and challenges.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.560
So it remains to be seen

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:18.560
if this technology actually
will be able to substitute

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.450
what other manufacturing
technologies can do.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.400
- [Narrator] A successful
orbital mission would not

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.580
only prove that a 3D printed
rocket can reach orbit,

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:27.780
it would also demonstrate

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.440
that Relativity's new 3D
printing technology can be used

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.933
to build reliable cost effective rockets.

00:07:35.906 --> 00:07:37.506
- This is gonna be a big rocket.

